\section{Result columns}{

SQLite is dynamically typed, so the Arrow type of a result column is decided from the values:
the first value that is not \code{NULL} decides,
and integers are widened to \code{double} if a real number turns up in the first chunk.
A column whose first chunk holds only \code{NULL} values takes the type of its declaration,
and a column without a declaration that only ever holds \code{NULL} is of the Arrow null type.
Once the first chunk has been fetched the types are fixed;
later values of another storage class are converted by SQLite's own rules,
with one warning per column.\tabular{ll}{
   \emph{SQLite value or declared type} \tab \emph{Arrow type} \cr
   integer, or INTEGER affinity \tab \code{int64} \cr
   real, or REAL and NUMERIC affinity \tab \code{double} \cr
   text, or TEXT affinity \tab \code{utf8} \cr
   blob, or BLOB affinity \tab \code{binary} \cr
   \code{DATE} with \code{extended_types = TRUE} \tab \code{date32} \cr
   \code{TIME} with \code{extended_types = TRUE} \tab \code{time64[us]} \cr
   \code{DATETIME} and \code{TIMESTAMP} with \code{extended_types = TRUE} \tab \code{timestamp[us, UTC]} \cr
   \code{NULL} only, no declared type \tab \code{null} \cr
}


SQLite integers are 64-bit, so integer columns are always \code{int64};
nanoarrow converts these to \code{double} by default,
use \code{bit64::integer64()} as the target type to keep the full range.
Dates, times and timestamps are parsed like the data frame path does it
when \code{extended_types = TRUE}, see \code{\link[=SQLite]{SQLite()}}.
